readings.c2s1:
¶

{{smaller block|Their ''unintelligence'', numbers, and fluctuating association prevented them from anticipating and following out any uniform and systematic measures. ''Sir W. Hamilton.''}}

'''unintelligent''' (un-in-tel′i-je̤nt), ''a.'' Not intelligent. {{smaller|(''a'') Not possessing or not proceeding from intelligence.}}
{{smaller block|What the stream of water does in the affair is neither more nor less than this: by the application of an ''unintelligent'' impulse to a mechanism previously arranged . . . by intelligence, an effect is produced, viz. the corn is ground.
{{right|''Paley'', Nat. Theol., ii.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Not knowing; not having acute mental faculties; not showing intelligence; dull.}}
{{smaller block|''Unintelligent'' persons that want wit or breeding.
{{right|''Sir M. Hale.''}}}}

'''unintelligently''' (un-in-tel′i-je̤nt-li), ''adv.'' In an unintelligent manner; without reason; dully.

'''unintelligibility''' (un-in-tel″i-ji-bil′i̤-ti), ''n.'' The character of being unintelligible.
{{smaller block|I omitted, . . . in the Introduction to the Abbot, any attempt to explain the previous story, or to apologize for ''unintelligibility.'' {{right|''Scott'', Abbot, I. 8.}}}}

'''unintelligible''' (un-in-tel′i-ji-bl), ''a.'' Not intelligible; not capable of being understood. ''Jer. Taylor'', Rule of Conscience, i. 21.

'''unintelligibleness''' (un-in-tel′i-ji-bl-nes), ''n.'' Unintelligibility. ''Bp. Croft.''

'''unintelligibly''' (un-in-tel′i-ji-bli), ''adv.'' In an unintelligible manner; so as not to be understood. ''Locke.''

'''unintentional''' (un-in-ten′sho̤n-al), ''a.'' Not intentional; not designed; done or happening without design.
{{smaller block|It is to be observed that an act may be ''unintentional'' in any stage or stages of it, though intentional in the pre-}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|ceding: and, on the other hand, it may be intentional in any stage or stages of it, and yet ''unintentional'' in the succeeding.
{{right|''Bentham'', Introd. to Morals and Legislation, viii. 12.}}}}

¶
'''unintentionality''' (un-in-ten-sho̤-nal′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''unintentional'' + ''-ity''.] The character of being unintentional; absence of design or purpose.
{{smaller block|''Unintentionality'' with respect to the event of the action, unconsciousness with regard to the circumstances.
{{right|''Bentham'', Introd. to Morals and Legislation, xvii. 11.}}}}

'''unintentionally''' (un-in-ten′sho̤n-a̤l-i), ''adv.'' Without design or purpose.

'''uninteressed†''' (un-in′tėr-est), ''a.'' Uninterested.
{{smaller block|That true honour and ''unintressed'' respect which I have always paid you. {{right|''Dryden'', Troil. and Cres., Ep. Ded.}}}}

'''uninterested''' (un-in′tėr-es-ted), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not interested; not having any interest or property in something specified; not personally concerned: as, to be ''uninterested'' in business.—'''2.''' Not having the mind or the passions engaged: as, to be ''uninterested'' in a discourse or narration.
{{smaller block|The greatest part of an audience is always ''uninterested'', though seldom knowing. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
='''Syn.''' See ''disinterested''.

'''uninteresting''' (un-in′tėr-es-ting), ''a.'' Not interesting; not capable of exciting interest, or of engaging the mind or passions: as, an ''uninteresting'' story or poem.
{{smaller block|Mrs. Henfrey . . . was, to all strangers, an absolutely ''uninteresting'' woman; but her family knew her merits.
{{right|''Jean Ingelow'', Fated to be Free, xviii.}}}}
='''Syn.''' {{smaller|Dull, tiresome, tedious, wearisome.}}

'''uninterestingly''' (un-in′tėr-es-ting-li), ''adv.'' In an uninteresting manner.

